1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,320
It's April, eighteen fifty.

2
00:00:04,783 --> 00:00:08,783
You're standing in a muddy street in Independence, Missouri.

3
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:11,890
Behind you is a wagon packed to the roof.

4
00:00:12,350 --> 00:00:16,110
In front of you are two thousand miles of Oregon Trail.

5
00:00:16,550 --> 00:00:20,150
No highway. No gas stations. No bathrooms.

6
00:00:20,617 --> 00:00:24,697
Welcome to the least glamorous road trip in history.

7
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:29,310
And here is the part the cartoons skip: you will not be riding.

8
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:33,590
You'll be walking beside that wagon for four to six months.

9
00:00:34,050 --> 00:00:37,410
That's roughly four million steps, give or take a blister.

10
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:42,730
Some people did it for land. Some, for gold. Some, for a better life.

11
00:00:43,183 --> 00:00:47,343
And by one estimate, at least twenty thousand people never arrived.

12
00:00:47,783 --> 00:00:52,103
You probably think you know what killed them. You're probably wrong.

13
00:00:52,550 --> 00:00:54,870
So let's pack the wagon and find out.

14
00:00:56,667 --> 00:01:00,027
First question: why would a sane person do this?

15
00:01:00,483 --> 00:01:05,763
Start with the sales pitch. Free farmland in a green valley called the Willamette.

16
00:01:06,217 --> 00:01:11,097
This very year, Congress will promise a married couple six hundred and forty acres.

17
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:13,950
A single man gets half of that.

18
00:01:14,417 --> 00:01:19,617
Now, that valley was not empty. The Kalapuya people lived there.

19
00:01:20,083 --> 00:01:24,723
Nobody asked them, and a treaty with them would not be signed until eighteen fifty-five.

20
00:01:25,183 --> 00:01:27,263
The brochures leave that part out.

21
00:01:27,717 --> 00:01:30,837
Then there's California, where gold has just been found.

22
00:01:31,283 --> 00:01:36,803
In eighteen forty-nine, about thirty thousand people headed west, mostly for California.

23
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:42,130
In eighteen fifty, by one count, it was fifty-five thousand.

24
00:01:42,583 --> 00:01:48,183
So you won't be alone. You're joining the slowest traffic jam in American history.

25
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:53,850
Over the whole era, historians guess that hundreds of thousands took this road.

26
00:01:54,317 --> 00:01:58,237
Why so vague? Because nobody was counting at a toll booth.

27
00:01:58,683 --> 00:02:02,443
For comparison, today you would rent a truck and call it a long weekend.

28
00:02:04,233 --> 00:02:07,033
Now, shopping day. And it is not cheap.

29
00:02:07,483 --> 00:02:11,643
A proper outfit costs roughly eight hundred to twelve hundred dollars.

30
00:02:12,083 --> 00:02:16,003
That includes a year of supplies, because you can't grow a crop on the way.

31
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:19,650
In today's money, that's more than thirty thousand dollars.

32
00:02:20,117 --> 00:02:22,677
First, the wagon. Forget the cartoons.

33
00:02:23,117 --> 00:02:26,317
The bed is about ten feet long and four feet wide.

34
00:02:26,783 --> 00:02:31,423
Over it, a canvas cover stretched on five or six curved wooden hoops.

35
00:02:31,883 --> 00:02:35,563
Your entire life has to fit in something the size of a big closet.

36
00:02:36,017 --> 00:02:38,577
And no, this is not a Conestoga wagon.

37
00:02:39,017 --> 00:02:44,457
The Conestoga was heavy, with a swayback shape, and it rarely went past the Missouri River.

38
00:02:44,917 --> 00:02:50,357
Pioneers used a lighter wagon with straight sides. The nickname was "prairie schooner."

39
00:02:50,817 --> 00:02:53,617
Now the engine. Oxen, or mules?

40
00:02:54,083 --> 00:02:58,643
Mules are faster. Oxen pull more, and they're less likely to stampede.

41
00:02:59,083 --> 00:03:06,043
At Fort Laramie in eighteen fifty, someone counted over thirty-six thousand oxen, and about seventy-five hundred mules.

42
00:03:06,483 --> 00:03:12,003
A few years later, Army captain Randolph Marcy priced an eight-ox team at about two hundred dollars.

43
00:03:12,450 --> 00:03:18,050
For two thousand miles, he says, young oxen will outlast mules.

44
00:03:18,517 --> 00:03:23,877
The lesson of the trail, so far: the fastest option is rarely the one that arrives.

45
00:03:24,317 --> 00:03:27,357
Nobody is selling extended warranties out here.

46
00:03:29,167 --> 00:03:35,407
Now, food. And here we get help from a classic manual, Marcy's The Prairie Traveler.

47
00:03:35,850 --> 00:03:40,170
It came out in eighteen fifty-nine, so you're a little early. But the advice holds up.

48
00:03:40,617 --> 00:03:44,297
His list, per grown person, for about a hundred and ten days:

49
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:47,550
One hundred and fifty pounds of flour.

50
00:03:48,017 --> 00:03:49,937
Twenty-five pounds of bacon.

51
00:03:50,383 --> 00:03:53,903
Fifteen pounds of coffee, and twenty-five of sugar.

52
00:03:54,350 --> 00:03:58,270
That's the menu. Bread, bacon, coffee. For months.

53
00:03:58,717 --> 00:04:01,757
Plus some cattle that walk along with you, and are also lunch.

54
00:04:02,217 --> 00:04:06,697
Everything else is a gamble. How many shovels? How many spare wheels?

55
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:10,350
And remember, every pound you pack, an ox has to pull.

56
00:04:10,817 --> 00:04:15,857
A wagon like yours can carry up to about twenty-five hundred pounds, in theory.

57
00:04:16,317 --> 00:04:19,677
In practice, travelers aimed for sixteen to eighteen hundred.

58
00:04:20,117 --> 00:04:25,317
So the rocking chair, the mirror, and the heavy furniture all have to go. Or go later.

59
00:04:25,783 --> 00:04:29,623
Many families did dump things along the road, to save their animals.

60
00:04:30,083 --> 00:04:34,803
One pioneer woman, Amelia Knight, threw out jars, buckets, and all her pickles.

61
00:04:35,250 --> 00:04:40,530
It's a hard truth of the trail: when you're an ox, a pickle is a very heavy thing.

62
00:04:42,333 --> 00:04:44,333
Then there's the guidebook problem.

63
00:04:44,783 --> 00:04:50,063
One famous book was The Emigrants' Guide to Oregon and California, by Lansford Hastings.

64
00:04:50,517 --> 00:04:52,997
It was published in eighteen forty-five.

65
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:57,450
Hastings told readers to bring a good gun. Fair enough.

66
00:04:57,917 --> 00:05:02,717
He also described a shortcut across the desert west of the Great Salt Lake.

67
00:05:03,183 --> 00:05:06,143
That description was only a few sentences long.

68
00:05:06,583 --> 00:05:09,463
And Hastings had never actually traveled that route.

69
00:05:09,917 --> 00:05:12,957
In eighteen forty-six, the Donner Party tried it.

70
00:05:13,417 --> 00:05:20,937
Snow trapped them in the Sierra Nevada. Of eighty-seven people, about forty-eight survived.

71
00:05:21,383 --> 00:05:24,823
It's the darkest story of the trail, and we won't dwell on it.

72
00:05:25,283 --> 00:05:30,883
The lesson is smaller, and more useful: check whether the author has actually been there.

73
00:05:31,350 --> 00:05:34,310
Which, by the way, is also a good rule for YouTube videos.

74
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:41,860
Now, the trail itself. Your alarm clock is a trumpet, or a rifle shot, around four in the morning.

75
00:05:42,317 --> 00:05:46,397
Breakfast. Round up the animals. Hitch the wagons.

76
00:05:46,850 --> 00:05:51,730
At about seven, the wagon master shouts, "Wagons roll!"

77
00:05:52,183 --> 00:05:53,223
And then you walk.

78
00:05:53,683 --> 00:05:56,323
The average is twelve to fifteen miles a day.

79
00:05:56,783 --> 00:06:01,663
That's about a half marathon, every day, for months, while herding cattle.

80
00:06:02,117 --> 00:06:05,477
At midday, there's a one-hour break called "nooning."

81
00:06:05,917 --> 00:06:09,037
Around six, the wagons form a circle.

82
00:06:09,483 --> 00:06:12,123
That circle is mostly a corral for the animals.

83
00:06:12,583 --> 00:06:16,343
Out on the plains, there are almost no trees.

84
00:06:16,783 --> 00:06:22,303
So what do you burn for dinner? Dried buffalo dung. They called it "buffalo chips."

85
00:06:22,750 --> 00:06:26,910
Someone has to collect it. Usually, women and children.

86
00:06:27,350 --> 00:06:31,590
Dinner: bacon, bread, and coffee, cooked over burning dung.

87
00:06:32,050 --> 00:06:37,170
Historian Lillian Schlissel studied the diaries and letters of one hundred and three women.

88
00:06:37,617 --> 00:06:44,737
They cook, they drive oxen, they care for children, and some give birth, all on the road.

89
00:06:45,183 --> 00:06:50,303
Amelia Knight wrote on April twenty-third: "Dreary times, wet and muddy."

90
00:06:50,750 --> 00:06:55,310
She was pregnant with her eighth child, who was born days after the family arrived.

91
00:06:55,750 --> 00:06:57,430
Not exactly a vacation.

92
00:06:57,883 --> 00:07:04,123
There is no phone to scroll. Your screen time is the horizon, and the back of an ox.

93
00:07:05,933 --> 00:07:07,693
Then come the rivers.

94
00:07:08,150 --> 00:07:10,550
Few of the big rivers have a bridge.

95
00:07:11,017 --> 00:07:14,537
Sometimes you float the wagon across on a makeshift raft.

96
00:07:14,983 --> 00:07:20,663
Sometimes you pay a ferry. In eighteen fifty, one party paid four dollars a wagon on the Platte.

97
00:07:21,117 --> 00:07:25,277
At one crossing, Knight counted more than three hundred wagons waiting their turn.

98
00:07:25,717 --> 00:07:28,837
Her group built a ferry out of rope and wagon beds.

99
00:07:29,283 --> 00:07:31,443
The women and children crossed last.

100
00:07:31,883 --> 00:07:36,443
On a steamboat, one of her oxen jumped overboard and swam across by itself.

101
00:07:36,883 --> 00:07:39,203
It was the only one with a plan.

102
00:07:39,650 --> 00:07:44,690
But rivers were no joke. Drowning at river crossings was a real cause of death.

103
00:07:45,150 --> 00:07:49,630
And in summer, the opposite problem: water holes dried up, and oxen died.

104
00:07:50,083 --> 00:07:51,923
Remember that word: water.

105
00:07:52,383 --> 00:07:56,783
Marcy, years later, would advise boiling water from stagnant pools.

106
00:07:57,250 --> 00:08:00,850
Good advice. Whether anyone followed it is another question.

107
00:08:02,667 --> 00:08:05,707
Because the biggest killer on the trail was not a person.

108
00:08:06,150 --> 00:08:07,430
It was cholera.

109
00:08:07,883 --> 00:08:13,323
Cholera is a bacterium. It spreads through water and food contaminated by human waste.

110
00:08:13,783 --> 00:08:19,063
Now picture a campsite with thousands of strangers, one creek, and no toilets.

111
00:08:19,517 --> 00:08:23,837
In those early gold rush years, it struck the outfitting towns every spring.

112
00:08:24,283 --> 00:08:27,803
Then it followed the trail, campground by campground.

113
00:08:28,250 --> 00:08:31,290
You could be healthy in the morning and dead by noon.

114
00:08:31,750 --> 00:08:37,110
Nobody knew what caused it. People treated it with camphor and laudanum.

115
00:08:37,550 --> 00:08:41,470
One emigrant counted seventy-one graves at a single campground.

116
00:08:41,917 --> 00:08:46,877
In eighteen fifty, by one count, cholera killed thousands.

117
00:08:47,317 --> 00:08:52,357
The Bureau of Land Management says at least twenty thousand people died on the Oregon Trail.

118
00:08:52,817 --> 00:08:56,977
Cholera, accidents, drownings, and other illnesses combined.

119
00:08:57,417 --> 00:09:01,497
Estimates vary a lot, so treat any number as a ballpark.

120
00:09:01,950 --> 00:09:04,590
Most of those graves are unmarked.

121
00:09:05,050 --> 00:09:10,410
One historian, John Unruh, even argued that the trail was probably not much deadlier than staying home.

122
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:15,810
Because in those same years, cholera was killing thousands in cities like Saint Louis.

123
00:09:16,250 --> 00:09:21,290
The most dangerous thing on the trail wasn't a person. It was a bucket of water.

124
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:28,060
Now, the scene you were waiting for. Wagons in a circle, arrows in the air.

125
00:09:28,517 --> 00:09:30,277
Let's check the numbers.

126
00:09:30,717 --> 00:09:38,237
Unruh counted, between eighteen forty and eighteen sixty, three hundred and sixty-two emigrants killed in clashes with Native people.

127
00:09:38,683 --> 00:09:42,843
And four hundred and twenty-six Native people killed in the same clashes.

128
00:09:43,283 --> 00:09:46,803
Over twenty years. Along a road used by hundreds of thousands.

129
00:09:47,250 --> 00:09:51,330
Those figures are tentative, and probably low. But they're not thousands.

130
00:09:51,783 --> 00:09:53,623
Compare that with cholera.

131
00:09:54,083 --> 00:09:59,843
Even the National Park Service says wagon trains were attacked far less often than the movies suggest.

132
00:10:00,283 --> 00:10:03,723
And most encounters, says the Park Service, were peaceful.

133
00:10:04,183 --> 00:10:09,143
And Unruh concluded that more Native people were killed by emigrants than the other way around.

134
00:10:09,583 --> 00:10:14,623
Many emigrants began the trip in needless fear, and sometimes fired first.

135
00:10:15,083 --> 00:10:19,243
Amelia Knight's daughters hid in the wagon the first time they saw Native people.

136
00:10:19,683 --> 00:10:22,963
By May second, she wrote, "Children are getting used to them."

137
00:10:23,417 --> 00:10:27,337
Still, the fear was real, and it shaped the whole trip.

138
00:10:27,783 --> 00:10:32,663
So the movie got one thing right: there was danger. It just got the proportions wrong.

139
00:10:34,467 --> 00:10:36,787
But the trail was not empty land.

140
00:10:37,250 --> 00:10:39,730
It crossed the homelands of many nations.

141
00:10:40,183 --> 00:10:47,623
Many Native people helped. They ran ferries, offered food, and guided travelers across dangerous rivers.

142
00:10:48,083 --> 00:10:51,443
Sometimes for payment, sometimes for free.

143
00:10:51,883 --> 00:10:57,963
They traded, too. They wanted livestock, clothing, metal tools, and mirrors.

144
00:10:58,417 --> 00:11:01,297
But a wagon train is not a quiet guest.

145
00:11:01,750 --> 00:11:07,350
Emigrant livestock stripped the grass for several miles on each side of the trail.

146
00:11:07,817 --> 00:11:12,057
The traffic disrupted the buffalo migrations that Plains peoples depended on.

147
00:11:12,517 --> 00:11:16,117
And cholera followed the trail into Native camps and villages.

148
00:11:16,583 --> 00:11:23,463
In September eighteen fifty-one, the United States held a council at Horse Creek, east of Fort Laramie.

149
00:11:23,917 --> 00:11:28,957
The goal was to legalize emigrant passage through Native lands, and reduce violence.

150
00:11:29,417 --> 00:11:35,817
The United States promised yearly payments for fifty years. As ratified, Congress cut that to ten.

151
00:11:36,283 --> 00:11:41,563
So when you picture the trail from the wagon seat, remember there was a very different view from the other side.

152
00:11:42,017 --> 00:11:44,737
Their story did not end where the wagon trail did.

153
00:11:46,533 --> 00:11:49,733
By midsummer, you reach Independence Rock, in Wyoming.

154
00:11:50,183 --> 00:11:55,703
A priest called it the "Great Register of the Desert," because of all the names carved there.

155
00:11:56,150 --> 00:11:59,190
Today, there are more than five thousand names.

156
00:11:59,650 --> 00:12:03,410
Next comes South Pass, the easy way over the Rockies.

157
00:12:03,850 --> 00:12:08,890
It's a valley about twelve miles wide. A wagon can cross it without any climbing drama.

158
00:12:09,350 --> 00:12:15,270
Then comes the hard part: dry stretches, tired oxen, and the Columbia River still ahead.

159
00:12:15,717 --> 00:12:17,957
Near the end, you face a choice.

160
00:12:18,417 --> 00:12:23,217
Float down the Columbia River by boat, or take the Barlow Road around Mount Hood.

161
00:12:23,683 --> 00:12:30,803
Until the Barlow Road opened in eighteen forty-six, the overland trail effectively ended at a town called The Dalles.

162
00:12:31,250 --> 00:12:37,490
The Barlow Road is a toll road. Five dollars per wagon, plus ten cents per head of livestock.

163
00:12:37,950 --> 00:12:42,030
It includes Laurel Hill, a descent of roughly two thousand feet.

164
00:12:42,483 --> 00:12:46,003
Wagons were chained by the wheels and lowered with ropes.

165
00:12:46,450 --> 00:12:51,330
Over the years, the ropes wore chutes in the hill as deep as seven feet.

166
00:12:51,783 --> 00:12:55,223
It was a one-way road, with a toll booth and a very steep hill.

167
00:12:57,033 --> 00:13:00,553
The trail officially ends at Oregon City, Oregon.

168
00:13:01,017 --> 00:13:04,217
But let's finish with Amelia Knight, in eighteen fifty-three.

169
00:13:04,683 --> 00:13:09,083
Her diary ends with these words: "This is the journey's end."

170
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:15,390
Her husband traded two yoke of oxen for half a section of land, and a small log cabin.

171
00:13:15,850 --> 00:13:18,810
That's three hundred and twenty acres, for two yoke of oxen.

172
00:13:19,250 --> 00:13:22,530
Her eighth child was born just days after they arrived.

173
00:13:22,983 --> 00:13:26,423
In eighteen sixty-nine, the railroad changed everything.

174
00:13:26,883 --> 00:13:31,203
An emigrant could go from Omaha to the Pacific in less than two weeks.

175
00:13:31,650 --> 00:13:36,930
The wagon trail faded. But about three hundred miles of ruts are still there.

176
00:13:37,383 --> 00:13:39,303
So what was the Oregon Trail, really?

177
00:13:39,750 --> 00:13:44,950
Not a western movie. A long, slow commute, on foot, by ordinary people.

178
00:13:45,417 --> 00:13:49,417
Where the most dangerous thing was a bucket of water, not an arrow.

179
00:13:49,883 --> 00:13:53,163
And where the "empty land" at the end was somebody's home.

180
00:13:53,617 --> 00:14:00,657
For many settlers, the gamble paid off. For the people already there, it was the start of a very hard chapter.

181
00:14:01,117 --> 00:14:04,077
Which brings us back to that muddy street in Independence.

182
00:14:04,517 --> 00:14:08,197
Would you walk four million steps for a farm in someone else's valley?

183
00:14:08,650 --> 00:14:11,850
Tell me in the comments: what's the first thing you'd leave behind?
